\begin{rmk}\label{comp}
We compare results on $\gZ(\wg^\vartheta,[0])$ and $\gZ(\wg^\vth,t^{-1})$. \\[.2ex]
{\sf (i)} The former algebra is always Poisson-commutative. For the latter, the statement is proven under the condition that 
$\ind\g_{(0)}=\rk\g$ \cite{fo}. Most probably, this condition holds for all reductive Lie algebras, see \cite[Conj.\,3.1]{MZ23}.  \\[.3ex]
{\sf (ii)} 
If $\g_{(0)}$ satisfies even stronger conditions, then 
$\gZ(\wg^\vth,t^{-1})$ is a polynomial ring by \cite[Thm.\,8.2\,\&\,Prop.\,8.3]{fo}. 
However, 
if $\gS(\g_{(0)})^{\g_{(0)}}$ is not a polynomial ring, then $\gZ(\wg^\vth,t^{-1})$ is not either, see \cite[Thm.\,8.2(ii)]{fo}. There are examples such that $\gS(\g_{(0)})^{\g_{(0)}}$
does not have an algebraically independent set of generators \cite{Y-imrn}.  In contrast, Theorems~\ref{g-twist} and \ref{twist-inf} apply to all reductive Lie algebras asserting that $\gZ(\wg^\vartheta,[0])$ is a polynomial ring. 
\end{rmk}


