\vspace{4 pt}
\textbf{Modeling the Perturbed Vortex }In cylindrical coordinates, $(r,\phi,z)$, the vector field of an axisymmetric zero-helicity vortex, \textit{e.g.}, the magnetic field of an FRC, may be represented analytically by the Soloviev equilibrium or Hill's vortex: 
\begin{gather}
    \textbf{B}_{0} = B_0\left( r z/z_s^2,\ 0, \  1-2r^2/r_s^2-z^2/z_s^2\right),
    \label{eq: field lines without perturbation}\\
    \psi_{0} = (B_0/2)\ r^2 \left(1-r^2/r_s^2-z^2/z_s^2\right).
\end{gather}
$\textbf{B}$ can describe magnetic fields for plasma confinement or velocity fields in fluids, depending on context. $B_0$ is the vector field strength scaling. Without perturbation, the flux function $\psi_{0}$ and azimuthal angle, $\phi$ uniquely label field lines. We perturb the vortex by $\textbf{B}=\textbf{B}_0+\delta\textbf{B}$,
\begin{equation}
    \delta\textbf{B} = -\alpha B_0(kz\cos{\phi},-kz\sin{\phi},kr\cos{\phi}),
    \label{eq: perturbation approx}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $\alpha>0$ is a free parameter controlling the perturbation’s field strength. $k>0$ is the axial wavenumber of the perturbation. The ratio of the field strength of a perturbation to the vortex field is $\sim\alpha k (r_s+z_s).$  The generality of the perturbation and validity of the vortex model is discussed in later sections and supplement \cite{supp}.

With the addition of an odd-parity perturbation, the previous unique labeling fails because the system is no longer axisymmetric. A unique labeling for every field line, a modified flux function (MFF), is still possible \cite{Ahsan1}. Written in Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$, where $x=r\sin\phi,\ y=r\cos\phi,\ z=z$. we have,
\begin{gather}
    \psi = (B_0 r_s^2/2)\cdot \textbf{u}^2(J-\textbf{v}^2)  
    \label{eq:psi}\\
   \text{where,}\quad \textbf{u}=\left(x,\ y-\alpha kz_s^2,\ 0\right)/r_s,
   \label{eq: u}\\
    \textbf{v}=\left(x/r_s,\ y/r_s+(\alpha kr_s/3)(z_s^2/r_s^2+1),\ z/z_s\right),
    \label{eq: v}\\
    \text{and}\ J=1-(\alpha k r_s /3)^2\left(z_s^2/r_s^2+1\right)\left(2z_s^2/r_s^2-1\right).
    \label{eq: J}
\end{gather}

If we translate the co-ordinate $\textbf{r}\rightarrow\textbf{r}-\alpha k z_s^2\ \hat{y}$, the cylindrical coordinate azimuthal angle shifts to
\begin{gather}
\phi\rightarrow\varphi=\cot^{-1}\left[(y-\alpha k z_s^2)/x\right].
\label{eq:phi}
\end{gather}

In this shifted coordinate system, \textit{via} straightforward calculation of $\nabla\psi$, it can be shown that the relationship between the vector field and modified flux function has the usual form,   $\textbf{B}=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\varphi$, thereby establishing the modified flux function's connection to physical flux. A field line stays stuck in the same $\psi$ curved surfaces and $\varphi$ planes. This can also be readily seen from $\textbf{B}\cdot\nabla\psi=0$ and $\textbf{B}\cdot\nabla\varphi=0$. 
Thus, \textit{for every point on a field line, the pair $(\psi,\varphi)$ remains constant.} This was also shown in \cite{Ahsan1}. Thus, the significance of the pair $(\psi,\varphi)$ is that they can help label a field line and work as a stand-in for the unperturbed flux function and azimuthal angle.

\vspace{4 pt}
\textbf{Topological Classifications } An important topological parameter from \cite{Ahsan1} is
\begin{align}
    \alpha_{c} = \left(kz_s\sqrt{1+2z_s^2/r_s^2}\right)^{-1}.
    \label{eq: critical strength}
\end{align}
Work done in \cite{Ahsan1} shows that at $\alpha = \alpha_c$, field lines in the $y$-$z$ plane undergo a phase transition and new non-smooth separatrix forms. However, for $\alpha \geq \alpha_c$, flux surfaces and field lines with the same $\psi$ become disconnected. This ruins the possibility of unique labeling for connected lines and surfaces, along with considerably more complicated topology. We therefore limit our work to $0 < \alpha < \alpha_c$, a reasonable assumption for small perturbations.

In Fig. \ref{fig:fluxsurface}, $\psi$ is increased while $\alpha<\alpha_c$ is kept fixed. After a critical value of $\psi$,  there is a transition from toroidal topology to simply connected topology, as marked by the sudden appearance of critical points on these surfaces. The exact value for $\psi$ and the shape of the region where the topological class of these surfaces changes is of interest. This transition surface, denoted the \textit{inner separatrix}, can be calculated by finding out when flux surfaces start intersecting the critical points ($\textbf{B}(\textbf{r})=0$). It is shown as the red circle -- the O-point null line-- in Fig. \ref{fig:fluxsurface}(c). The first such intersection happens when $\psi=\psi_-$ at point $(0,y_+,0)$. Doing the calculation gives us a closed form for $\psi_-$ given in Eq. \eqref{eq: psi_pm}.
\begin{gather}
     \psi_\pm\equiv\psi(0,y_\mp,0)
    ,\ y_\pm=\frac{-\alpha k r_s^2\pm r_s\sqrt{\alpha^2 k^2r_s^2+8}}{4}
    \label{eq: psi_pm}\\
    \text{Inner separatrix,}\ S_{in} \equiv \{\textbf{r}: \psi(\textbf{r})=\psi_- \}
    \label{eq: innerseparatrix}\\
   \text{Outer separatrix,}\ S_{out} \equiv \{\textbf{r}: \textbf{v}(\textbf{r})^2=J\}
\end{gather}
As can be readily checked, $S_{out}$ is a subset of the $\psi=0$ surface. With these notations, we can now state the theorems to classify the topology of the flux surfaces and the field lines.

\noindent \textit{Theorems: For $0<\alpha<\alpha_c$,}

\textit{1. Any pair $(\psi,\varphi)\in(-\infty,\psi_+)\times[0,2\pi)$ and uniquely label connected field lines via one-to-one function.}

\textit{2. For $\psi\in(-\infty,0)$, all field lines and flux surfaces are open and fully outside of $S_{out}$.}

\textit{3. For $\psi\in(0,\psi_-)$, all flux surfaces and field lines are closed and strictly inside $S_{out}$ but outside the $S_{in}$ and are closed. The flux surfaces are topologically torus.}

\textit{4. For $\psi\in(\psi_-,\psi_+)$, all flux surfaces and field lines are closed and strictly inside $S_{in}$. The flux surfaces are topologically spherical.}
\begin{figure}[!ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{psi_vs_a.jpg}
    \caption{Categorization of compact flux surfaces, $0<\psi<\psi_+$, for all $\alpha<\alpha_c$. Surfaces on blue region, red region and dashed black line are respectively torus, simply connected and  transition surfaces. Here $r_s=z_s$.}
    \label{fig:psi_vs_a}
\end{figure}

Details of the  proof are in the supplement materials \cite{supp}. $\alpha$ and $k$ can get arbitrarily close to $0$, thus simply connected surfaces are guaranteed to exist. For a spherical vortex ($z_s = r_s$), even a small perturbation $\alpha = 0.2\alpha_c$ or $\alpha kr_s\approx 0.11$ makes $\approx 67\%$ of compact flux surfaces simply connected, rising to $\approx 90\%$ at $\alpha = 0.4\alpha_c$ or $\alpha kr_s\approx 0.23$ and increases monotonically (see Fig \ref{fig:psi_vs_a}). Thus, simply connected flux surfaces are the majority, even for small perturbations. 

\vspace{4 pt}
\textbf{Particle Motion } In a fluid vortex, the particle/fluid motion is the same as the field lines and is guaranteed to stick to the flux surfaces discussed. Thus, particle motion has already been addressed in the previous section in the context of fluids. In the context of the $\textbf{B}$ representing FRC magnetic fields, particle motion deviates strongly from the field lines. The wide variety of motions can be attributed to nonlinearity such as  $\mu$ non-conservation \cite{Glasser_2002}. As representative cases, we examined, \textit{via} numerical simulation, whether electron motion exhibited simply connected patterns -- closed crescent-shaped surfaces -- in an FRC perturbed by static odd-parity RMF. 

A parameter commonly used to demarcate between fluid-like and kinetic FRC plasmas is \textit {s} $\equiv0.3r_s/\rho$, where $\rho$ is the particle gyro-radius at $r = r_s$ and  $z = 0$. (For $s > 10$ the FRC is generally considered fluid; for $s< 10$ it is generally considered kinetic.) This criterion is misleading because particles near the O-point null line or the X-point nulls experience a lower magnetic field, have larger $\rho$, and are in a region of greater field curvature, hence have a far lower local value \textit{s}. In the simulations described below,  \textit{s} $\sim 800$ but the local value of \textit{s}, \textit{$s_l$}, may be less than 1 in places along a particle's trajectory.

