\noindent
\textbf{Main results.} \
Our main contribution (Theorem~\ref{Thm:1}) provides an explicit formula for all vertices of $\operatorname{1\text{-}CUT}(n)$ in terms of a new combinatorial object: the \emph{alternating cycle function}. This function generates integer sequences with distinctive palindromic and fractal properties that directly encode the binary structure of cut polytope vertices. The formula reveals that the encoded vertices, when appropriately normalized, exhibit an almost-linear ordering along the line $y = x - \frac{1}{2}$, exposing an unexpected  regularity in the polytope's structure.

Beyond vertex generation, this result illustrates a deeper interplay between discrete geometry, binary encoding, and probabilistic models. The alternating cycle function itself may have broader relevance for the study of  0/1-polytopes and their underlying symmetries.

\medskip

