\section{Comparisons with~\cite{Creci:2024wfu}}\label{eq:comparison_Creci}
%------------------------------------------------------------------------
%------------------------------------------------------------------------

In~\cite{Creci:2024wfu}, the authors have recently derived the tidal corrections to the dynamical and radiative sector in ST theories, at leading order in the different types of tidal deformability coefficients. In the context of our work, this corresponds to the leading-order contributions that are linear in the $0^{th}$ order coefficients, namely $(\lambda_a^{(0)},\mu_a^{(0)},\nu_a^{(0)},c_a^{(0)})$. 

A direct comparison between their results and ours is not straightforward. In our approach, the matter fields $\mathfrak{m}$ are coupled with the Jordan-frame metric $g_{\mu \nu}$, whereas in Ref.~\cite{Creci:2024wfu}, the authors perform a conformal transformation of the form
%
\begin{align}
\hat{g}_{\mu \nu} = A(\hat{\varphi})g_{\mu \nu}
\end{align}
%
where $A(\hat{\varphi})$ is the conformal factor, $\hat{g}_{\mu \nu}$ denotes the Jordan-frame metric and $g_{\mu \nu}$ the Einstein-frame metric. After such a transformation, the action within the Einstein frame takes the form 
%
\begin{align}
S_{ST}^{(E)} = \int \dd^4x \frac{c^3\sqrt{-g}}{16 \pi G}\left[ R-2 \, g^{\mu \nu}\, \partial_{\mu}\hat{\varphi}\,\partial_{\nu}\hat{\varphi}\right] + S_{\text{matter}}\left[\mathfrak{m}, A(\hat{\varphi})g_{\mu \nu}\right]\,.
\end{align}
%
Since they couple the matter fields with the Einstein-frame metric, mapping our tidal deformability coefficients to those used in~\cite{Creci:2024wfu} introduces a mixing between the different types of coefficients. Such a mapping is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:mapping_TLN}.
%
\hspace{0.5cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
	\hline 
	\textbf{Our notation} & \textbf{Notation from Ref.~\cite{Creci:2024wfu}} \\[5pt]
	\hline 
	\hline  
	 $\lambda_a^{(0)}$ & $-\frac{\Delta_0}{A_0^3} \, \lambda^{\ell=1}_S$  \\[10pt]
	 $\mu_a^{(0)}$ & $-\frac{\Delta_0}{A_0}\,\left[\lambda^{\ell=2}_{S}+ c^2 \left(\frac{\partial ln A}{\partial \hat{\varphi}}(\hat{\varphi}_0)\right)^2 \lambda^{\ell=2}_{T} +2 c^2\left(\frac{\partial ln A}{\partial \hat{\varphi}}(\hat{\varphi}_0)\right)\lambda^{\ell=2}_{ST}  \right]$ \\[10pt]
     $\nu_a^{(0)}$ & $-A_0\sqrt{\Delta_0}\,\left[\lambda^{\ell=2}_{ST}+\left(\frac{\partial ln A}{\partial \hat{\varphi}}(\hat{\varphi}_0)\right)\lambda^{\ell=2}_{T} \right] $ \\[10pt]
	 $c_a^{(0)}$ & $A_0^3\, \lambda^{\ell=2}_T$ \\[8pt]
\hline
\end{tabular}
\captionof{table}{Translation map for tidal deformability coefficients. \label{tab:mapping_TLN}}
\end{center}
%
Using these relations, we are in full agreement with their results for the dynamical sector in the center-of-mass frame and for circular orbits. We also find consistency in the gravitational and scalar fluxes in the center-of-mass frame. However, since only the 1PN gravitational flux is needed in our calculation to construct the full 2PN flux, the comparison is restricted to the leading-order dipolar scalar tidal contribution. Finally, we have a few discrepancies in the scalar flux for circular orbits and in the SPA phase in the DD regime, as presented respectively in Eqs.~(B.11) and~(B.17) of Ref.~\cite{Creci:2024wfu}. However, after performing some consistency checks between their expressions, we suspect that the differences might come from an original typographic error in their Eq.~(B6).

