\section{Introduction}

E-commerce has become a cornerstone of global trade, with digital platforms enabling businesses of all sizes to reach international customers. In recent years, online retail has seen explosive growth worldwide: Business-to-Consumer (B2C) e-commerce sales have increased from approximately \$1.5 trillion in 2014 to over \$6 trillion in 2024, exceeding the GDPs of Japan and Germany 
%surpassing the nominal GDPs of major economies such as Japan and Germany in the same year
\citep{WorldBank2019ecommerce,WorldBankEGATE}. %\citep{WorldBank2019ecommerce,Statista2023,PrecedenceResearch2025,WorldBankEGATE}. 
Within this boom, cross-border e-commerce (CBEC) has emerged as a rapidly growing segment, increasing from around \$300 billion in 2015 to over \$1.2 trillion in 2024 \citep{MarketResearchFuture2025}.
%Estimates indicate that cross-border online trade was around \$300 billion in 2015 and surged to approximately \$1.2 trillion in 2024 \citep{WorldBank2019ecommerce, MarketResearchFuture2025}, underscoring the global nature of today’s digital marketplaces.
%Estimates indicate that cross-border online trade was around \$300 billion in 2015, rising to nearly \$900 billion by 2020 \citep{WorldBank2019ecommerce}. Recent industry reports indicate that the global B2C CBEC market has surged to approximately \$1.2 trillion in 2024 \citep{MarketResearchFuture2025}, %and is projected to reach around \$13 trillion by 2034 \citep{MarketResearchFuture2025}, underscoring the global nature of today’s digital marketplaces.
