These two risk measures are fundamental to credit risk evaluation. For example, widely used credit risk measure, such as the Probability of Default (PD), is directly given by $r_1(l)$, and the Loss Given Default (LGD) can be computed as
\begin{equation*} \label{eq:LGD}
	\text{LGD} =\frac{\mathbb{E}[L(l) \mid L(l) > 0]}{l} = \frac{\mathbb{E}[L(l)]}{l\,\Pr\{L(l)>0\}} = \frac{r_2(l)}{l\,r_1(l)}.
\end{equation*}
Moreover, suppose we are interested in the probability that the loss exceeds a threshold level $\xi$, where $0<\xi<l$. Then,
%\begin{equation*}
%	\Pr\{L(l)>\xi\} = \Pr\{(l - rY)^+ > \xi\} 
%	= \Pr\{l - rY > \xi\} 
%	= \Pr\{Y < (l-\xi)/r\} 
