\subsubsection{Asymptotic Analysis for $r_1(l)$ Estimation %$\hat{r}_1(l)$
}\label{sec:asymptotic_r1}

In this subsection, we establish the asymptotic properties of the estimated default probability \( \hat{r}_1(l) \), a key metric in credit risk assessment for SCF.
%We begin our asymptotic analysis by focusing on the estimation of $r_1(l)$, which corresponds to the probability of defaut.  
Given any \(\bx\), the default probability $r_1(l)$ satisfies:
\begin{equation*}
	r_1(l) = \Pr\{L(l) > 0\} =  \Pr\{Y(\bx) < l/r\}= F_{Y}\bigl(\,l/r\!\mid\!\bx\bigr),
\end{equation*}
%Accurately estimating \( r_1(l) \) across the loan decision space \( l \in [0, \bar{l}] \) is crucial for informed lending strategies, as it directly influences loan sizing decisions and risk management practices. However, achieving reliable and stable estimates is challenging due to the continuous variation of $l$ and the need to approximate the underlying sales distribution \( Y(\bx) \) accurately.

In the QRGMM framework, ignoring the Monte Carlo sampling error, $r_1(l)$ is essentially approximated by $\hat{r}_1(l)=F_{\hat{Y}}(\,l/r|\bx)$, where $F_{\hat{Y}}(\cdot|\bx)$ is the CDF of the generative model $\hat{Y}(\bx)$. The accuracy of \( \hat{r}_1(l) \) thus hinges on the convergence of \( F_{\hat{Y}}(y | \bx) \) to the true CDF \( F_Y(y | \bx) \). 

In SCF context, by understanding the distribution of $Y(\bx)$, lenders can determine the appropriate loan level $l$ such that we can achieve a desired PD. To support this loan sizing decision making, it is not enough to have mere pointwise convergence of $F_{\hat{Y}}(\,l/r|\bx)$ for a fixed $l$. Instead, a stronger form of uniform convergence is required. Uniform convergence of $F_{\hat{Y}}(y|\bx)$ for all $y\in[0,\infty)$ ensures the uniform convergence of $\hat{r}_1(l)$ for all $l\in[0,\bar{l}]$.  This uniform convergence is a stronger and more challenging requirement, as it ensures consistent accuracy across the entire loan decision space, addressing a gap in existing literature that often overlooks the responsive interplay between loan levels and risk measures.


Our objective is to prove both pointwise and uniform convergence of \( \hat{r}_1(l) \) to \( r_1(l) \). These theoretical guarantees are essential for ensuring the reliability of QRGMM in SCF. Leveraging results from \cite{hong2023learning}, we proceed to establish the uniform convergence of $F_{\hat{Y}}(y|\bx)$, which in turn guarantees the uniform convergence of $\hat{r}_1(l)$ over the loan decision space.


