\end{proof}

This theorem confirms the uniform consistency of the QRGMM estimator for $r_3(l)$. The convergence guarantee is facilitated by the boundedness and smoothness of the integrand $g_l(\cdot)$, and the controlled behavior of the truncation threshold $a(l)$. These structural conditions are naturally satisfied in SCF applications,

The uniform convergence of \( \hat{r}_3(l) \), matching the asymptotic behavior of \( \hat{r}_1(l) \) and \( \hat{r}_2(l) \), underscores the robustness of QRGMM in the SCF context. This theorem, coupled with the flexibility of \( g_l(\cdot) \) and $a(l)$, enhancing the applicability of \( r_3(l) \), allows \( r_3(l) \) to adapt to diverse risk management needs while maintaining estimation consistency. In summary, the uniform convergence results of \( \hat{r}_1(l) \), \( \hat{r}_2(l) \), and \( \hat{r}_3(l) \) ensures reliable risk assessment across varying loan levels, enhancing decision-making in SCF applications such as adaptive loan sizing and risk mitigation. 


\section{Extension of QRGMM for Practical Applicability in SCF Risk Management}
\label{sec:extension_qrgmm}

In the previous chapters, we focused on the QRGMM under relatively simple model assumptions, notably linear quantile regression. While these assumptions facilitated theoretical derivations and provided valuable insights into convergence properties, they may be restrictive for real-world SCF scenarios. In practice, the relationships governing sales—especially in cross-border e-commerce—are often complex, involving intricate interactions among products, stores, and other contextual factors. Linear models may struggle to capture such combinational effects, potentially undermining the accuracy and effectiveness of distributional predictions required for robust SCF risk management.

To overcome these limitations, this chapter integrates advanced techniques from recommendation systems, specifically Deep Factorization Machines \citep[DeepFM,][]{guo2017deepfm}, with the QRGMM framework. This integration allows us to model non-linear, intricate feature interactions and improve the realism and accuracy of generated sales distributions. We term this extended model as QRGMM$^\dagger$, highlighting its advanced capability to handle complex combinational features and non-linear relationships, essential for robust SCF applications.



\subsection{Overview of Related Techniques}
\label{sec:related_techniques}

This subsection introduces the key building blocks for our extended model. We first discuss Matrix Factorization \citep[MF,][]{lee2000algorithms} and Factorization Machines \citep[FM,][]{rendle2010factorization}, fundamental techniques in recommendation systems that enable efficient modeling of latent and combinational features. We then present Deep Factorization Machines, which combine FM with deep learning technique to capture second-order and higher-order feature interactions. Lastly, the Quantile Regression Neural Network \citep[QRNN,][]{cannon2011quantile} provides a remarkable approach for extending quantile regression from linear models to neural networks, making it an ideal glue to seamlessly integrate DeepFM with the QRGMM framework.

\subsubsection{Matrix Factorization (MF)}
Matrix Factorization is a foundational technique in recommendation systems and collaborative filtering. It decomposes a large, sparse interaction matrix (e.g., user-item ratings) into the product of two lower-rank latent factor matrices: $R \approx U^\top V,$
where \( R \) is the observed rating matrix, \( U \) and \( V \) are matrices of user and item latent factors, respectively. The model is trained by minimizing reconstruction error while incorporating regularization to prevent overfitting:
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{U,V}\sum_{(i,j)\in \mathcal{K}} (r_{ij}-U_i^\top V_j)^2+\lambda(\|U\|^2+\|V\|^2),
\end{equation*}
where \( \mathcal{K} \) is the set of observed entries and \(\lambda\) is a regularization parameter. The learned model $U^\top V$ can then be used to predict unknown ratings, effectively filling in the missing entries in the matrix (see Figure~\ref{fig:mf}).

\begin{figure}[!ht]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{Figures/Matrix_Factorization_diagram_01_v2}
	\hfill
	\includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{Figures/Matrix_Factorization_diagram_02_v2}
	\caption{Illustration of the Matrix Factorization process.}
	\label{fig:mf}
\end{figure}

In the SCF context, we can analogize users and items to entities such as stores and products, and ratings to observed sales. By identifying latent factors underlying these interactions, MF provides insight into complex, hidden relationships that simpler linear models may fail to capture.



\subsubsection{Factorization Machines (FM)}
While MF focuses on a user-item dyad, Factorization Machines extend this idea to a broader class of features and their interactions. FM efficiently captures pairwise feature interactions by introducing latent vectors for each feature:
\begin{equation*}
	\hat{y}(\mathbf{x}) = w_0+\sum_{i=1}^n w_i x_i + \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=i+1}^n \langle \mathbf{v}_i, \mathbf{v}_j \rangle x_i x_j,
\end{equation*}
where \(\mathbf{v}_i \in \mathbb{R}^k\) is the latent vector for the \(i\)-th feature and \(\langle \cdot,\cdot \rangle\) denotes the inner product. %FM generalizes MF by incorporating any number of features, capturing their combinational effects even in sparse conditions.
One key insight of FM is that it models the coefficients of interactions between different features as the inner product of their latent vectors, effectively decomposing their interactions through these coefficients. 

\begin{figure}[!ht]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{Figures/MF2FM_notitle_v2}
	\caption{From Matrix Factorization to Factorization Machines.}
	\label{fig:FM}
\end{figure}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:FM}, compared to MF, FM provides a more flexible structure that can incorporate a broader range of features, making it suitable for capturing complex relationships in diverse datasets. Additionally, compared to traditional polynomial kernel-based methods like Support Vector Machines (SVM), FM demonstrates superior generalization capabilities in sparse settings. The dense parametrization of SVMs relies on direct observations of feature interactions, which are often unavailable in sparse data, whereas FM can effectively estimate parameters even under such conditions (for more details, see \cite{rendle2010factorization}).


\subsubsection{Deep Factorization Machines (DeepFM)}
Deep Factorization Machines combine the strengths of FM with deep neural networks to model both low-order and high-order feature interactions. The FM component efficiently learns second-order feature interactions, while the deep neural network component captures complex, higher-order interactions without the need for extensive manual feature engineering. The structure of DeepFM is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:deepfm}.

