\subsubsection{Risk Measures Approximation}

In this synthetic experiment, for a specified $\bx$, the data-generating process of \( Y(\bx) \) is known, enabling us to analytically compute true risk measures as benchmarks for assessing the performance of QRGMM$^\dagger$ and CWGAN approximations. In our setup, the loss function \( L(l) \) defined in equation \eqref{eq:loss} is used, and the maximum loan amount \( \bar{l} \) is set to the 99th percentile of \( rY(\bx) \), with net unit revenue \( r = 1 \). Loan levels \( l \) are evaluated at 100 equally spaced points across \( [2500, \bar{l}] \). 

We compute four true risk measures: \( r_1(l) \) (probability of default), \( r_2(l) \) (expected loss), and \( \text{LGD}(l) \) (loss given default), as specified in Section \ref{sec:riskformulation}, and \( r_3(l) = \mathbb{E}[((l - rY(\bx))^+)^2] \), a specific form of the generalized risk measure highlighting severe losses.  QRGMM and CWGAN approximations are calculated over 100 replications, each with 10,000 samples, and their means are compared to true values and estimations using the true test data samples averaged over 100 replications. These results are depicted in Figure \ref{fig:risk_measures_plot}, with subplots for \( r_1(l) \), \( r_2(l) \), \( \text{LGD}(l) \), and \( r_3(l) \).

