\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper introduced a novel SCF risk management framework that leverages generative model to provide a full distributional understanding of sales, developing a unified analytical framework that simultaneously addresses credit risk assessment and loan size determination—objectives traditionally treated separately. By integrating QRGMM—a quantile-regression-based generative modeling method—with DeepFM to capture complex feature interactions, we have developed a flexible approach capable of estimating entire conditional distributions in complex SCF settings. This allows practitioners to move beyond conventional point estimations and simple risk classifications, offering a data-driven, distribution-oriented perspective on SCF risk.

Building on the linear quantile regression framework of \cite{hong2023learning}, we establish rigorous asymptotic guarantees for three key risk measures, which serve as universal building blocks for deriving a wide range of risk metrics. Numerical experiments on synthetic data confirm that QRGMM accurately and stably approximates the underlying distribution, surpasses CWGAN in unconditional and conditional distribution matching, and recovers risk measures that align closely with known truths across the range of loan level space, greatly facilitating loan-sizing decision making in an intuitive way. On real-world datasets, QRGMM demonstrates practical applicability, accurately estimating quantiles and generating distributions that mirror real sales patterns.

In summary, this work represents an initial exploration of the use of generative AI for operations management, advancing existing SCF risk management methodologies by employing generative models that provide richer distributional insights. However, much work remains to be done in this area. Future research should extend the framework to handle time-series data with seasonal effects and tackle challenges related to high-dimensional datasets. Additionally, while this study focuses on a single product for an SME, future efforts could explore loan portfolio problems by accounting for intricate interdependencies among products, including complementarity and substitution effects. And applications in other domains such as financial risk management and supply chain optimization could also be investigated. By continuing to refine these generative distributional approaches, the ultimate goal is to support more informed, resilient, and data-driven decision-making in various fields of operations management.


%
%Our study pioneers the application of generative models to SCF risk management tailored to the unique challenges of CBEC by developing a unified analytical framework that simultaneously addresses credit risk assessment and loan size determination—objectives traditionally treated separately. By leveraging the abundant, granular sales data from e-commerce platforms and recent advances in generative AI, our approach transcends conventional financial statement-based methods. Our contributions are threefold. First, we extend quantile-regression-based generative metamodeling (QRGMM) to capture the complete conditional distribution of product sales, enabling robust estimation of key risk measures—default probability and expected loss—across a continuum of loan levels. Second, building on the linear quantile regression framework of \cite{hong2023learning}, we establish rigorous asymptotic guarantees, including uniform convergence and explicit convergence rates, which serve as universal building blocks for deriving a range of regulatory and tail risk metrics. Third, we address the combinatorial complexity of e-commerce sales dynamics by integrating QRGMM with DeepFM, effectively capturing nonlinear interactions among covariates and overcoming the limitations of traditional point prediction methods.



