	First, we assume that $T$ is an ideal triangulation. If $\gamma\in T$ is not inside a self-folded triangle of $T$, then $b_{\gamma,T}(\ell)$ is defined by a sum of contributions from all intersections of $\gamma$ and $\ell$ as follows:
	Such an intersection contributes $+1$ (resp., $-1$) to $b_{\gamma,T}(\ell)$ if a segment of $\ell$ cuts through the quadrilateral surrounding $\gamma$ as shown in the left (resp., right) diagram of Figure \ref{PB8YMzV20uMT}.
