\section{Frustration-free inner-limit derivations and systems of projections}\label{Sec:FFDer}

This section recalls the definition of frustration-free derivations and introduces the related concept of frustration-free systems of projections. Basic properties of the latter are established and examples are supplied.

\iffalse
Inner-limit derivations over $C^\ast$-algebras are well studied \cite{BratteliCMP1975,BratteliBook0} objects:

\begin{definition}
    Let $\bm h=\{h_x\}_{x \in X}$ be a net of self-adjoint elements from a separable unital $C^\ast$-algebra $\Aa$. We associate to $\bm h$ a derivation $\delta_{\bm h}$ with (maximal) domain
    \begin{equation}
        \Dd_{\bm h} : = \{a \in \Aa \, | \, [h_x,a]:=h_x a - a h_x \ \mbox{is a Cauchy net} \},
    \end{equation}
and action
    \begin{equation}
        \delta_{\bm h} : \Dd_{\bm h} \to \Aa, \quad \delta_{\bm h}(a) : = \lim \, [h_x, a]. 
    \end{equation}
 We call such $\delta_{\bm h}$ an inner-limit derivation.
\end{definition}

The domain of an inner-limit derivation is closed under addition and multiplication, hence it is automatically a subalgebra. The spectrum of each $h_x$ is a compact subset of the real line, and, for unital algebras, we can adjust these spectra such that 
    \begin{equation}\label{Eq:SpecCond}
        \min \, {\rm Spec} \, h_x =0,
    \end{equation} 
by adding multiples of unity, and this will be assumed to be the case. Such adjustments do not affect the domain or the action of the inner-limit derivation.

Derivations are interesting if they supply a dynamics over the $C^\ast$-algebra. Necessary and sufficient conditions for this to be the case can be found in \cite{BratteliCMP1975,BratteliBook0,BratteliBook1,BratteliBook2} and many other sources. We are also primarily interested in situations where the $C^\ast$-algebra admits an action by an infinite discrete group:

\begin{definition}
    Let $\Aa$ be a $G$-$C^\ast$-algebra. We say that $\delta$ is a $G$-derivation if $\delta$ commutes with the group action, that is, its domain is invariant against the group action and the action of $\delta$ on this domain commutes with the action of the group.
\end{definition}

