\subsection{Angular Integrals}
Armed with the $\bsym{\pi}_{\boldsymbol{\xi}}^{[k]}$ kernels, we're in a position to evaluate the angular integrals that yield the 4th order contributions of the $\bld{Z}_{\boldsymbol{\xi}}^{[k]}$ terms.
 With $\beta=\beta(t^r)$, we have when $k=0$
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\label{eq:23}
\bld{Z}_{\boldsymbol{\xi}}^{[0],(4)}(t,t^{r})=-\frac{\varkappa^{4}}{4}\int^{2\pi}_{0}\int^{\pi}_{0} 
 \lt[\frac{(1-\beta \cos\theta)\bsym{\beta}-\beta(\cos\theta-\beta)\nh}{1-\beta \cos \theta}\rt]\sin\theta \drm\theta\drm\phi.
\end{split}
\end{align}
Here, the superscript ${}^{(4)}$ denotes that this is the $4$th order contribution to $\bld{Z}^{[0]}_{\bxi}$. 
When evaluating the integral over $\phi$, 
we observe that the only dependence is on the unit vector $\nh$ in the directions perpendicular to the motion. 
The integrations of these components will vanish and for the parallel component we simply obtain a factor of $2\pi$. 
Denoting the parallel component  by $\parallel$, we find
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\label{eq:24}
Z^{[0],(4)}_{\parallel}(t,t^{r})=
-\varkappa^{4}\frac{\pi}{2}\int^{\pi}_{0} 
 \lt[\beta-\frac{\beta(\cos\theta-\beta)\cos \theta}{1-\beta \cos \theta}\rt]\sin\theta \drm\theta
\end{split}
\end{align}
where we evaluated $\bsym{\beta}\cdot \nh= \beta\cos\theta $ with $\beta=\bsym{\beta}(t^{r})\cdot\be$. 
% Making a substitution $x=1-\beta \cos \theta$ such that $d\theta = dx/\beta \sin \theta$, and $\cos \theta=(1-x)/\beta$
The integral can be carried out explicitly, yielding
\begin{align}
\label{eq:25}
