Let $S$ be a topological cryptogroup and $N$ be a full normal subcryptogroup of $S$. Consider the natural mapping  $\psi : \underset{x  \, \mapsto \, (xN)^*}{\overset {S \longrightarrow S/N}{}}$. Now, we define a topology on $S/N$ as : a subset $U$ of $S/N$ is open in $S/N$ if and only if $\psi^{-1}(U)$ is open in $S$. In this case, $\psi$ is a quotient mapping and $S/N$ is a quotient space.

Similar to the \cite[Theorem 5.10]{mp}, we can prove the following result.

\begin{theorem}
If $S$ is a band of topological groups and $N$ is a full normal subcryptogroup of $S$, then $S/N$ is a band of topological groups. 
\end{theorem}

%\begin{proof}
%By Theorem \ref{quotient crypto}, $S/N$ is a cryptogroup. We now show that the natural map  $\psi : \underset{x  \, \mapsto \, xN}{\overset {S \longrightarrow S/N}{}}$ is open. For this, let $U$ be an open set in $S$. Now $\psi^{-1}\psi(U) = \psi^{-1}\{xN : x \in U\} = \bigcup \{xN : x \in U\}$. Let $z \in \bigcup \{xN : x \in U\}$. Then $z \in xN$, for some $x \in U$. This implies $x^{-1}z \in N$ and $x^0 = z^0$. Then $x^{-1}z \in (N\cap H_{_x})$ and which implies $z \in x(N \cap H_{_x})\subseteq \bigcup \{y (N \cap H_{_y}) : y \in U \cap H_{_y}\}$. Now let $t \in \bigcup \{y(N \cap H_{_y}) : y \in U \cap H_{_y}\}$. Then $t \in y(N\cap H_{_y})$, for some $y \in U \cap H_{_y}$. This implies that $y^{-1}t \in N\cap H_{_y}$ and $y^0 = t^0$. It follows that $t \in yN \subseteq \bigcup \{xN: x \in U\}$. Hence $\bigcup \{xN : x \in U\} = \cup \{y(N \cap H_{_y}) : y \in U \cap H_{_y}\}$. Now we consider the set $\{x(N \cap H_{_x}) : x \in U\cap H_{_x}\}$. Now for any $x \in U$, $U \cap H_{_x}$ is open in $H_{_x}$. Also, $H_{_x}$ being a topological group, $\{x(N \cap H_{_x}) : x \in U \cap H_{_x}\} = (U \cap H_{_x})(N \cap H_{_x})$ which is open in $H_{_x}$ and so in $S$. It follows that $\bigcup \{xN: x \in U\}$ is open in $S$. So $\psi$ is open. Now we show $S/N$ is a topological semigroup. Let $G$ be an open set in $S/N$ and $xN, yN \in S/N$ with $xN \cdot yN \in G$. Then $xy \in \psi^{-1}(G)$. Then there exist open sets $P$ and $Q$ containing $x$ and $y$ respectively in $S$ such that $PQ \subseteq \psi^{-1}(G)$. Then $\psi$ being open, $\psi(P)$ and $\psi(Q)$ are open sets containing $xN$ and $yN$ respectively in $S/N$ satisfying $\psi(P) \psi(Q) \subseteq G$. So $S/N$ is a topological semigroup. Now we verify that $\gamma : \underset{xN  \, \mapsto \, x^{-1}N}{\overset {S/N \longrightarrow S/N}{}}$ is continuous. Let $W$ be open in $S/N$. Now  $p \in \psi^{-1}(\gamma^{-1}(W))$ if and only if $pN \in \gamma^{-1}(W)$ if and only if $(pN)^{-1} = p^{-1}N \in W$ if and only if $p^{-1} \in \psi^{-1}(W)$ if and only if $p \in (\psi^{-1}(W))^{-1}$. Hence $\psi^{-1}(\gamma^{-1}(W)) = (\psi^{-1}(W))^{-1}$. Now $W$ being open, $\psi^{-1}(W)$ is open in $S$ and this implies $(\psi^{-1}(W))^{-1}$ is open in $S$, i.e., $\psi^{-1}(\gamma^{-1}(W))$ is open in $S$. It follows that $\gamma^{-1}(W)$ is open in $S/N$. Therefore, $S/N$ is a topological cryptogroup. Let $G_{_1}$ be an open set in $S/N$ and $rN \in G_1$. Then $r \in rN \subseteq \psi^{-1}(G_1)$. Now $S$ being a band of topological groups, there is an open set $V_1$ in $S$ such that $r \in V_1 \subseteq \psi^{-1}(G_1) \cap H_{_r}$. This implies that $rN \in \psi(\psi^{-1}(G_1) \cap H_{_r}) \subseteq G_1 \cap \psi(H_{_r}) = G_1 \cap H_{_{rN}}$. Thus, by Theorem \ref{main theorem}, $S/N$ is a band of topological groups.
%\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
Let $S$ be a band of topological groups and $N$ be a full normal subcryptogroup of $S$. Then the following conditions are equivalent :

(i) $S/N$ is Hausdorff;

(ii) $\rho_{_N}$ is closed in $S\times S$;

(iii) $N$ is closed in $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(i) \Rightarrow (ii)$: Let $S/N$ be Hausdorff. Let $(a,b) \in \overline{\rho_{_N}}$. If possible, let $(a,b) \notin \rho_{_N}$. Then $(aN)^* \neq (bN)^*$. Since $S/N$ is Hausdorff, there exist open sets $U$ and $V$ containing $(aN)^*$ and $(bN)^*$ respectively in $S/N$ such that $U \cap V = \emptyset$. This implies $\psi^{-1}(U)$ and $\psi^{-1}(V)$ are open sets containing $a$ and $b$ respectively in $S$, where $\psi : S \longrightarrow S/N$ is the natural epimorphism. From this it follows that $\psi^{-1}(U) \times \psi^{-1}(V)$ is an open set containing $(a,b)$ in $S \times S$. Since $(a,b) \in \overline{\rho_{_N}}$, we must have $\psi^{-1}(U) \times \psi^{-1}(V) \cap \rho_{_N} \neq \emptyset$. Let $(x, y) \in \psi^{-1}(U) \times \psi^{-1}(V) \cap \rho_{_N}$. Then  $(xN)^* \in U$, $(yN)^* \in V$ and $(xN)^* = (yN)^*$. This implies $U \cap V \neq \emptyset$, a contradiction. Therefore, $(a,b) \in \rho_{_N}$ and hence $\rho_{_N}$ is closed.

$(ii) \Rightarrow (iii)$: Let $\rho_{_N}$ be closed in $S \times S$. Let $x \in \overline{N}$. If possible, let $x \notin N$. Then $(x^0)^{-1}x = x \notin N$. This implies that $(x^0, x) \notin \rho_{_N}$. Now $\rho_{_N}$ being closed, there is a basic open set $U_1 \times V_1$ containing $(x^0, x)$ in $S \times S$ such that $(U_1 \times V_1) \cap \rho_{_N} = \emptyset$. Since $\varphi : \underset{x  \, \mapsto \, x^0}{\overset {S \longrightarrow S}{}}$ is continuous, $\varphi^{-1}(U_1)$ is an open set containing $x$ in $S$. Then $\varphi^{-1}(U_1) \cap V_1$ is an open set containing $x$ in $S$. Now $x \in \overline{N}$ implies that $(\varphi^{-1}(U_{_1}) \cap V_{_1}) \cap N \neq \emptyset$. Let $p \in (\varphi^{-1}(U_{_1}) \cap V_{_1}) \cap N$. Then $p^0 \in U_{_1}$, $p \in V_1 \cap N$ and hence  $(p^0, p) \in (U_1 \times V_1) \cap \rho_{_N}$, a contradiction. Hence $x \in N$ and so $N$ is closed. 

$(iii) \Rightarrow (i)$: Let $N$ be closed. Let $(uN)^*, (vN)^* \in S/N$ with $(uN)^* \neq (vN)^*$. Then $(u,v) \notin \rho_{_N}$. This implies either $u^{-1}v \notin N$ or $u^0 \neq v^0$.  If $u^0 \neq v^0$, then $H_{_u}$ and $H_{_v}$ are disjoint open sets containing $u$ and $v$ respectively in $S$. Now, the natural mapping $\psi : S \longrightarrow S/N$ being open, $\psi (H_{_u})$ and $\psi (H_{_v})$ are disjoint open  sets containing $(uN)^*$ and $(vN)^*$ respectively in $S/N$. Hence $S/N$ is Hausdorff. On the other hand, if $u^{-1}v \notin N$, then $N$ being closed, there is an open set $G$ containing $u^{-1}v$ in $S$ such that $G \cap N = \emptyset$. Now, since $S$ is a topological cryptogroup, there exist open sets $U_2$ and $V_2$ containing $u$ and $v$ respectively in $S$ such that $U_2^{-1}V_2 \subseteq G$. Then $\psi (U_2)$ and $\psi (V_2)$ are open sets containing $(uN)^*$ and $(vN)^*$ respectively in $S/N$. If possible, let $\psi (U_2) \cap \psi (V_2) \neq \emptyset$ and let $(xN)^* \in \psi (U_2) \cap \psi (V_2)$. Then $(xN)^* = (pN)^*$ and $(xN)^* = (qN)^*$, for some $p \in U_2$ and $q \in V_2$. From this, we have $p^{-1}q \in U_2^{-1}V_2 \subseteq G$ and $p^{-1}q \in N$. This implies $G \cap N \neq \emptyset$, a contradiction. Hence $\psi (U_2)$ and $\psi (V_2)$ are disjoint open sets containing $(uN)^*$ and $(vN)^*$ respectively in 
$S/N$. Consequently, $S/N$ is Hausdorff. 
\end{proof}


