\section{Introduction}
In this paper, we study the %multi-measurement %multichannel 
spectral compressed sensing problem with multiple measurements vectors  (MMV) \cite{Cotter2005}, 
which aims to reconstruct the multi-measurement spectral sparse signals from partial observations. 
%The multichannel signals, also known as multiple measurement vectors (MMV) or multiple-snapshot signals,
%Multichannel spectrally sparse signal
Multi-measurement spectral sparse signals refer to multiple signals sharing the same sparse frequencies. These signals  
widely arises in applications such as power system monitoring \cite{Zhang2018,Gao2016}, wireless communication \cite{Barbotin2012}, target localization %and imaging 
in radar systems \cite{Potter2010}, and direction-of-arrival (DOA) estimation in array processing \cite{Krim1996}. The multi-measurement spectral sparse signal $\{\vx_l\}_{l=0}^{s-1}$ %, where $j\in\{0,1,\cdots,n-1\}$, 
is formed as:  
\begin{align*}
\vx_l(j) = \sum_{k=0}^{r-1} b_{k,l} e^{(\imath2\pi f_k-\tau_k)j}, %=\sum_{k=1}^r d_k\vb_{k}e^{(\imath2\pi f_k-\tau_k)t},
\numberthis\label{eq:signal_model}
\end{align*}
where $j\in\{0,1,\cdots,n-1\}$, $l\in\{0,1,\cdots,s-1\}$ , $n$ is the length of signal, $s$ is the number of measurement vectors, and $r$ is the joint spectral sparsity,   $\imath=\sqrt{-1}$, $f_k\in[0,1)$ is the $k$-th normalized frequency, $\tau_k$ is the $k$-th damping factor,  $b_{k,l}\in \C$ is the amplitude for the $k$-th sinusoids component at the $l$-th measurement. %, $n$ is the length of signal, $s$ is the number of measurement vectors, and $r$ is the joint spectral sparsity.  
%and $r$ is the joint spectral sparsity.
We stack the multiple signals $\{\vx_l\}_{l=0}^{s-1}$ into a matrix as $\mX_\star=[\vx_{0},\cdots,\vx_{s-1}]^T\in\C^{s\times n}$ and $\mX_\star$ can be reformulated as: %. Let $\vb_{k}=[b_{k,0},\cdots,b_{k,s-1}]^T\in\C^s$ and then $\mX_\star$ can be reformulated as:
%Define the multichannel signal matrix $\vX_\star=[\vx_{0},\vx_{1},\cdots,\vx_{n-1}]\in\C^{s\times n}$, and it can be reformulated as: 
\begin{align*}
\mX_\star=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}\vb_k\va(p_{k})^T, %=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}d_k\vb_k\va(p_{k})^T, 
\numberthis\label{eq:Ground_truth}%\in\S,
\end{align*}
where $\vb_{k}=[b_{k,0},\cdots,b_{k,s-1}]^T\in\C^s$, %$\vd_k=$ $d_k=\|\vd_k\|_2$, $\vb_k=\vd_k/d_k$, 
$\va(p_k)=[1,p_k,\cdots,p_k^{n-1}]^T\in\C^n$ and $p_k=e^{(\imath2\pi f_k-\tau_k)}$. % $\va_k[n]=[1,w_k,\cdots,w_k^{n-1}]^T\in\C^{n}$,  $w_k=e^{(\imath2\pi f_k-\tau_k)}$, $\circ$ denotes outer product and 
%$$
%    \S=\left\{\mZ|\mZ{=}\sum_{k=1}^{r}\vd_k\va_n(f_k,\tau_k)^T,f_k\in[0,1),\tau_k\in\C,\vd_k\in \C^s \right\}.
%$$

Due to hardware limitations such as sensor malfunction, sparse array design, and non-uniform sampling in the time domain, %and the fact that obtaining the complete set of sampling points is time-consuming, 
only a portion of the multiple signal ensemble can be observed in practice. %Additionally, the canonical full sampling on a uniform grid is inefficient due to the weakening effects caused by the damping factors $\{\tau_k\}$.
A natural task, therefore, is to recover the target matrix $\mX_\star$ from its partial observations, i.e.,
\begin{align*}
\mbox{Find}\quad\mX%\in\S
\quad\mbox{subject to}\quad\P_{\Omega}(\mX)=\sum_{(i,j)\in{\Omega}}\mX_\star(i,j)\ve_{\mathrm{s},i}(\ve_{\mathrm{n},j})^T,%\numberthis\label{eq:setup}
\end{align*}
where $\ve_{{\mathrm{s}},i}$, $\ve_{{\mathrm{n}},j}$ is the canonical basis of $\R^s$ and $\R^n$  respectively,  
%${\Omega}\subseteq[s]\times[n]$ 
${\Omega}\subseteq\{0,1,\cdots,s-1\}{\times}\{0,1,\cdots,n-1\}$ 
is the index set, {$m=|\Omega|$ is the number of observations,} %with cardinality as $m$, %where we denote $[s]$ as the set $\{0,1,\cdots,s-1\}$ and $[n]$ is similarly defined
 and
 $\P_{\Omega}$ is a projection operator. %which projects the signal $\mX$ onto this index set ${\Omega}$, and $\P_{\Omega}(\vZ)=\sum_{a\in{\Omega}}\la\vz,\ve_a\ra\ve_a$ for $\vz\in\C^n$.

 Gridless approaches were proposed to reconstruct multi-measurement spectrally sparse signals %from partial observations, 
 as seen in \cite{Yang2016a,Li2016,Zhang2018}. In particular, \cite{Yang2016a,Li2016} introduced an atomic norm minimization (ANM) framework with multiple measurement vectors, which leverages convex optimization to solve the problem. However, these convex approaches are computationally expensive for large-scale problems. To address these challenges, AM-FIHT \cite{Zhang2018} is proposed by lifting the multiple signal ensemble  $\mX_\star\in\C^{s\times n}$ to a Hankel matrix $\H(\mX_\star)\in\C^{s n_1 \times n_2}$ where $n=n_1+n_2-1$. The spectral sparsity of the signal is then captured through the low-rank structure of the Hankel matrix, expressed as: $$\H(\mX_\star)=\mP_{L}\bm{\Gamma}\mP_R^T,$$ where $\mP_{L}\in\C^{sn_1\times sr}, \bm{\Gamma}\in\C^{sr\times r}$ and $\mP_R\in\C^{n_2\times r}.$ %the lifted Hankel matrix 
 This reformulation converts the problem into a low-rank Hankel matrix completion task as follows
 \begin{align*}
\min_{\mX\in\C^{s\times n}}&\la \P_{\Omega}\(\mX-\mX_\star\),(\mX-\mX_\star)\ra
\\&\mbox{s.t.}~\rank(\H(\mX))=r,
\end{align*}
 which is solved using the fast iterative hard thresholding (FIHT) algorithm \cite{Cai2019}. While this approach reduces computational costs relative to convex methods, %its computational complexity, $O(snr\log(n)+snr^2)$, and storage complexity, $O(snr)$, 
 its computational complexity remains high, particularly when the number of multiple measurement vectors $s$ is large. 
