\subsubsection{Odd equally-distanced points starbursts}

The first example is a wave aberration given by:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:3star}
W  = 0.2 Z_{4}^{0}  + 0.2 Z_{3}^{3}.
\end{equation}

Figure \ref{fig:3stars} shows the wave aberration, Hessian determinant of $W$, and caustic pattern generated by \eqref{eq:3star}. We provide two Hessian determinant maps. In the left column, the colorbar is proportional to the Hessian determinant over all the pupil; however, in the right column, the colorbar is clipped to specific values around zero to reveal the structure of the Hessian determinant in close regions to the cusps of Gauss.  



The wave aberration preserves the 3-fold symmetry (Proposition~\ref{propA}) between the Hessian determinant map and the caustic pattern. 
Overall, there are $7$ cusps of Gauss, $3$ being fertile (saddle cusps generating near fold caustics), setting the planes of specular symmetry and giving rise to a three-point starburst pattern because of the caustic cusps associated with each of the three fertile cusps of Gauss. 

