The EWMCP is an important generalization of the classical \textit{Maximum Clique Problem} (MCP), with real-world applications across many domains, such as robotics, computer vision and materials science \citep{agapito2016accurate, ma2012maximum, san2015novel, san2013improved}. Since the MCP is 
 strongly \(\mathcal{NP}\)-hard, the same holds for the  EWMCP \citep{gary1979computers, karp2010reducibility}. 
The EWMCP is an important weighted generalization of the MCP. We refer the interested reader to other relevant weighted clique-related problems; see, for instance, \cite{CONIGLIO2021435,CORNAZ2017151,DELLEDONNE202139,FURINI2019112,FURINI202154,MATTIA202448,SANSEGUNDO201918}.

 Over the past decades, numerous exact approaches have been proposed in the literature to solve the EWMCP to proven optimality \citep{lei2015unified, prokopyev2009equitable, san2019new, shimizu2019branch, shimizu2020maximum}. The main state-of-the-art techniques are combinatorial branch-and-bound algorithms that efficiently explore the search space through an implicit enumeration \citep{san2019new, shimizu2019branch, shimizu2020maximum}. These algorithms rely on effective bounding techniques to limit the search space. 

There are two main upper bounds for the optimal value of the EWMCP, each forming the core of the bounding procedures in the exact algorithms proposed by \citet{san2019new} and \citet{shimizu2020maximum}. The first is based on a \emph{Linear Programming} (LP) relaxation of an \emph{Integer Linear Programming} (ILP) formulation for the EWMCP, while the second is a purely combinatorial upper bound that directly exploits the structure of feasible solutions to the EWMCP. Both upper bounds are formally defined in Section~\ref{sec:UB}. To the best of our knowledge, these two upper bounds were developed independently and simultaneously, and no comparison has ever been made in the literature.


% Such bounding techniques are based on a common strategy: they transform the original edge-weighted graph into a vertex-weighted one by assigning some edge-weights to the vertices.
% %thereby enabling the computation of upper bounds tailored to the \emph{Vertex-Weighted Maximum Clique Problem} (VWMCP). 
% Then the bounds are typically obtained by partitioning the graph into independent sets (i.e., computing a coloring) and summing the weights of the heaviest vertex in each color class (see, e.g., \citep{fang2016exact}). The way in which weights are assigned on vertices, starting from an edge-weighted graph, plays a crucial role in determining the tightness of the resulting bounds. In the approach of \citet{san2019new}, each edge-weight is split between its endpoints, and the induced vertex weights are used to compute the bound. In contrast, \citet{shimizu2020maximum} explore alternative policies for assigning weights to vertices, without relying on an initial splitting. Despite these methodological differences, both strategies share the same high-level structure: converting the edge-weighted clique problem into a vertex-weighted one to exploit known bounding techniques based on graph coloring.

This work aims to study, both theoretically and computationally, the strength of these two upper bounds. Our first theoretical result, presented in Section~\ref{sec:WCA}, shows that there exists a set of instances for which the ratio between either of the two upper bounds and the optimal value of the EWMCP is unbounded. Our second theoretical result, in Section~\ref{sec:THEOR_COMP}, provides a comparison between the two upper bounds by defining two families of instances in which either upper bound can be arbitrarily worse than the other. These results, while ruling out the possibility of obtaining any relative performance guarantee with respect to the optimal value of the EWMCP, offer insight into the structural properties of the graphs that influence the strength of the two upper bounds.


On the computational side, in Section~\ref{sec:COMP_COMP} we report extensive results on two sets of instances to empirically assess the relative strength of the two upper bounds. When the optimal value of the EWMCP can be computed, we also report the corresponding optimality gap. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:CONCL}, we draw the main conclusions, highlighting the structural properties of graphs that favor one upper bound over the other, and outlining directions for future research.


