 \section{Theoretical comparison of the two upper bounds with $\optimum$}
\label{sec:WCA}

In this section, we provide a sequence of EWMCP instances in which the ratio between either of the two upper bounds and the edge-weighted clique number becomes arbitrarily large as the number of vertices in the graphs tends to infinity.

%We now construct a family of graphs along with their edge-weight vectors, designed to illustrate the behavior of the upper bounds.
%To this end, we first introduce the EWMCP instances.
For every integer \( n \ge 1 \), let \( G^1_n = (V^1_n, E^1_n) \) be a graph with a vertex set containing \( 2n \) vertices, partitioned into two cliques \( C_1, C_2 \subseteq V^1_n \), where
\[
C_1 = \{1, 2, \dots, n\}, \qquad C_2 = \{n+1, n+2, \dots, 2n\}.
\]
The edge set of the graph includes all edges within the two cliques, each assigned an edge-weight of~1. Moreover, for each vertex \( u \in C_1 \), there is an edge \( \{u, n+u\} \) with edge-weight
\[
\bar{c} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2} - 1,
\]
and, by construction, the vertex \( n+u \) belongs to \( C_2 \). These edge-weights define the edge-weight vector \( \boldsymbol{c}^1_n \) for the graph \( G^1_n \).
