\section{Theoretical comparison between the two upper bounds}\label{sec:THEOR_COMP}

In this section, we provide two sequences of EWMCP instances in which the ratio between the two upper bounds, or its reciprocal, becomes arbitrarily large as the number of vertices in the graphs tends to infinity.


% In this section, we carry out a theoretical comparison between $\OSBound$ and $\JAPbound$. We describe two families of instances for which one bound can be arbitrarily worse than the other, in both directions. For the computation of the bounds, in these instances, we always use a coloring featuring a minimal number of colors. This choice is justified as the tightness of the bounds depends heavily on the quality of the coloring applied. Specifically, using unreasonable colorings — those with a number of colors significantly exceeding the minimum required — would result in an unfair and inconsistent comparison. Furthermore, it is unlikely that poor colorings would be employed in practical applications of these bounds.

