A coloring of the vertices of $G^3_n$ with the minimum number of colors is naturally given as $\mathscr{C}^3_n = \{I_1, I_2\}$.

The following proposition states that the ratio between the upper bound $\OSBound$  and $\JAPbound$  can be arbitrarily large.


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:example_japan_wins}
The sequence $\{(G^3_n,\boldsymbol c^3_n)\}$ of EWMCP instances is such that 
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\OSBtren}{\JAPtren} \rightarrow + \infty \quad   ~~\text{as}~~ \quad n \rightarrow +\infty,
\end{equation*}
where $\mathscr C^3_n$ is the coloring of $G^3_n$ defined above.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We first show that the optimal value of Model
\eqref{form:LP-italia-spagna}, namely $\OSBtren$, is $n$. 
We notice that, in any feasible solution of Model \eqref{form:LP-italia-spagna}, for every $u\in I_1$ and $v\in I_2$, Constraints \eqref{con:LP-italia-spagna_max-w} relative to the edge $e=\{u,v\}$ write as 
$$\pi_{I_1}\ge \sum_{e\in\delta(u)}\varrho_{eu},\qquad \pi_{I_2}\ge \sum_{e\in\delta(v)}\varrho_{ev}.$$

Adding up these constraints over all vertices therefore yields
\[
   n\bigl(\pi_{I_1}+\pi_{I_2}\bigr)
       \;\ge\;
       \sum_{e \in E} \ c_{e},
\]
where we used Constraint \eqref{con:LP-italia-spagna_splitting}.

Moreover, all edge-weights are equal to 1, so the sum on the right hand side equals $|E^3_n|=n^2$.
Consequently, $\pi_{I_1}+\pi_{I_2}\ge n$, and every feasible solution of Model\eqref{form:LP-italia-spagna} has value at least $n$.

This lower bound of $n$ on $\OSBtren$ is tight: if we assign each edge-weight wholly to its endpoint in $I_2$ and make the variable assignment $\pi_{I_1}=0,\ \pi_{I_2}=n$, all constraints of Model \eqref{form:LP-italia-spagna} are satisfied and the objective function equals $n$.

Hence
\[
  \OSBtren = n.
\]

\medskip
As far as the bound $\JAPtren$ is concerned, we observe that every vertex $u\in I_1$ is such that $\sigma_u=0$, since there are no independent sets with an index lower than $1$. Whereas every vertex $v\in I_2$ is such that $\sigma_v=1$, since it is connected only to vertices in $I_1$ with edges of weight $1$. Hence, we have:
$$\max\{\sigma_u\colon u\in I_1\}=0, \qquad \max\{\sigma_u\colon v\in I_2\}=1,
$$
giving the upper bound 
$$\JAPtren=1.$$
Finally, it follows that
$$
\lim_{n\to +\infty}\frac{\OSBtren}{\JAPtren}= \lim_{n\to+\infty}\frac{n}{1}=+\infty.
$$


\end{proof}

Note that the values of the two upper bounds in the examples studied in this section, and thus the conclusion of the two propositions, depend on the choice of a particular coloring of the vertices of the graphs $G^2_n$ and $G^3_n$. Nevertheless, our choices feature a minimum number of colors and the results hold with any coloring with this natural property.

