\subsection{SEY under multipacting resonance}
We now turn to the evaluation of SEY under multipacting resonance conditions. Multipacting resonance occurs when secondary electrons emitted from a surface are accelerated by an RF field such that their time of flight $\tau$ is synchronized with the RF period $T$~\cite{Shemelin2013, Haoran_2019}. The resonance order $N$ is given by $N = \tau / T$. When the SEY associated with a given resonance exceeds unity, the number of secondary electrons can increase with each RF cycle, potentially leading to an electron avalanche during a long RF pulse.

To identify resonance conditions, we begin with a specified initial RF phase \( \phi_0 \) and axial emission position \( z_0 \), and calculate the electron trajectory over multiple iterations. A convergence criterion is applied, requiring both the RF phase \( \phi \) at which the electron leaves the surface and the axial position \( z \) to stabilize within a tolerance of \( 1 \times 10^{-6} \) over ten consecutive iterations. 

