      \field{abstract}{Computing with high-dimensional vectors in a manner that resembles computing with numbers is based on Plate's Holographic Reduced Representation (HRR) and is used to model human cognition. Here we examine its hardware realization under constraints suggested by the properties of the brain's circuits. The sparseness of neural firing suggests that the vectors should be sparse. We show that the HRR operations of addition, multiplication, and permutation can be realized with sparse vectors, making an energy-efficient implementation possible. Furthermore, we propose a processor that has both data and instructions embedded in the same high-dimensional vector. The operation is highlighted with a sequence memory example.}
